  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. The Lao Federation of Trade Unions (LFTU) conducted a series of survey on impact of COVID-19 (Vientiane in 2020; national in October 2021). Based on the survey results, LFTU presented recommendations on how tripartite mechanism can mitigate the impact of COVID-19 at the stakeholders&apos; dialogue in November 2021, joined by the Lao National Chamber of Commerce (LNCCI) and the Ministry of Labour and Social Welfare (MLSW). The tripartite body has adopted a policy on the role of each body to protect workers in the pandemic and post-pandemic. 
2. The LFTU submitted a convincing recommendation to the MLSW and LNCCI on the ratification of ILO Conventions Nos. 155 and 187 after a series of ILO workshops in October 2020, aiming to raise understanding and awareness of these two conventions. The push for ratification was also echoed by the provincial union leaders who shared OSH challenges at the workplace and labour law enforcement at the provincial level, the urgent need to address these challenges and further dialogue for the ratification of Conventions Nos. 155 and 187. After receiving the demands of LFTU, similar to those of the employers, and having fully understood the context of the conventions, the government made a commitment to ratify these conventions. 
3. Following the country&apos;s DWCP review meeting last 23 September 2021, the LFTU&apos;s three (3) priority areas of work were supported by the MLSW and have been reflected in the revised LAOS PDR DWCP.  These priorities were:: (1) development of a joint 5-year capacity development strategy in order to strengthen impact and sustainability. Support these by annual activity and resourcing plans, (2) address the needs of women and men workers in the informal economy in all areas; and (3) ratification of the ILO Conventions on OSH, C155 and  C187. 
4. After a series of ILO training workshops, the LFTU and its affiliates have also contributed to successful  labour-management cooperation to recover from the COVID-19.  A surve The ILO convened consultations with LFTU for developing proposals. 

The ILO provided technical support for LFTU to conduct nation-wide survey on impact of COVID-19. 

The ILO provided technical support to LFTU to conduct trainings for capacity building (leadership and IT capacity). 

With contribution from fund by Japanese government, the ILO provided technical support to LFTU to produce a research paper (which will be finalized by November) on good practice (work place cooperation). The paper will be used for bipartite meeting.</narrative>
   </description>
